<KickoffSession
    documentTitle="The data has to reach the person. | Canada2080"
    description="Dr Erin Trochim on geospatial evidence, cognitive offloading, and four tests for community control, from her cleaned remarks at the Canada2080 kickoff."
    eyebrow="Sovereign AI · 1:20 p.m."
    headline="The data has to reach the person."
    name="Dr Erin Trochim"
    bio="Dr Erin Trochim is a geospatial AI and remote-sensing researcher. Her work connects place-based resilience, geospatial evidence, and critical adoption of AI."
    profileUrl="https://www.linkedin.com/in/erin-trochim/"
    podiumSrc="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5380.jpg"
    podiumAlt="Dr Erin Trochim on the screen, joining the kickoff remotely."
    photos={[
        {
            src: "/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5380.jpg",
            alt: "Dr Erin Trochim on the screen, joining the kickoff remotely.",
            caption: "On the screen.",
        },
    ]}
>
    <p class="lede">
        A vast amount of geospatial data exists. Very little of it reaches the
        person who needs it when something breaks.
    </p>
    <p>
        She joined as a Canadian living in Alaska, with perspectives of her
        own, and picked up the geospatial thread from the talk before hers.
    </p>
    <p>
        Her story was a door stuck closed. An earthquake can do it. So can a
        house built on permafrost, or a single frost heave, not only seasonally
        frozen ground. As a homeowner, she said, the geospatial record has
        often known about the movement for a long time. The question is whether
        that information gets back to the person and turns into action. Too
        often it goes straight to the insurer.
    </p>
    <p>
        NASA's NISAR products already track ground movement. The issue is not
        only whether the house is moving, but the ground around it. Did
        somebody know? Could they say so? And if they do, what happens next?
    </p>
    <p>
        Communities have plenty of data. From the jurisdictional side, much of
        it is still not connected, and that has been known for a long time.
        New systems need common protocols across people who govern themselves:
        individuals, municipalities, tribal nations, and state and federal
        governments, while research in those places is still underway. The
        design question is how authority stays with the participants.
    </p>
    <p>
        AI agents arrive with access to compute, data, and tools, and the
        coordination problem is already there. Cognitive offloading, as she
        used it, is the habit of handing thought to the model. The other half
        is being deliberate about who the system does more for, and keeping
        the ability to question a finding and to know how.
    </p>
    <p>
        Simple surveys may be public. Someone still publishes the finding, and
        someone still decides what happens next.
    </p>
    <p>
        For Canada in 2080, she sees room for new frameworks, and a lot in
        common with the conversations already underway in Alaska. Her work has
        been building information networks from community conversations, and
        folding community planning into the system itself. She offered four
        tests of control.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Contestability. Can the people, communities, or institutions
            affected contest or refuse a consequential decision?
        </li>
        <li>
            Dependency. Can participants operate, interpret, maintain, and
            negotiate without one vendor or one expert?
        </li>
        <li>
            Value intention. Are community assets strengthened or extracted?
        </li>
        <li>
            Adaptability. Can institutions and communities exit, provide,
            substitute, or repair?
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Her suggestion: the volume of data is also an opportunity. Start from
        how the system should function, what actions it performs, and the tacit
        knowledge people already hold. The data is easier to organize from
        there, and AI makes that planning more useful.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="transcript">
        <p class="transcript-note">Cleaned up transcripts.</p>
        <div class="transcript-columns">
            <div>
                <h3>Keynote</h3>
        <blockquote>
            <p>
                Thank you, everybody, and happy to be joining you as a Canadian
                living in Alaska. This morning, I have perspectives of my own.
            </p>
            <p>
                Canada is a massive place, and I appreciate that a lot,
                especially with my time studying and implementing some of my
                work here on this side of the border. But I appreciated the
                previous presentation's discussion on geospatial data. I want
                to tell you some stories about my work and the perspectives I'm
                bringing.
            </p>
            <p>
                I gave a presentation last week that resonated well in Alaska.
                It was about what happens when your door is stuck closed. This
                can happen for a variety of reasons. We have major earthquakes
                here that can disturb structures, and we also build houses on
                permafrost. It doesn't have to be our seasonally frozen ground
                that causes issues; it can also be a single frost heave.
            </p>
            <p>
                I can tell you right now, as a homeowner, what happens when you
                have to deal with it. From the geospatial perspective, we have
                known quite a bit for a long time, but whether that information
                makes it back to you and translated into action is a key
                perspective to consider, especially in this AI era.
            </p>
            <p>
                Right now, a lot of communities have data. We have
                analysis-ready products by NASA called NISAR that track ground
                movement. It's a bit tricky because it's not only about whether
                your house is moving, but also the area around it. The main
                idea is: Did somebody know? Could they tell you about it? And
                if they do tell you, what happens? Often, we go straight to
                deferring to insurance companies to insure this, but we
                frequently don't bring the information back to the homeowners
                and to the actions that need to happen.
            </p>
            <p>
                This is the point where I often want to start by centering our
                work, especially centering our thoughts around adopting AI and
                what our new information systems can look like in the future.
                The answer is that we have tons of data, but from the
                jurisdictional side, a lot of it isn't connected right now, and
                we've actually known this for quite some time.
            </p>
            <p>
                When thinking about these systems, you really have to consider
                how to develop common protocols across independently governed
                systems. We need to acknowledge the variety of participants,
                such as individuals, municipalities, tribal nations, and state
                and federal governments, while also recognizing that active
                research is still occurring in these spaces. How do we design
                these systems while understanding how authority is retained
                among participants?
            </p>
            <p>
                In AI, we need shared protocols, especially as we start to
                introduce new tools like AI agents, which means they have
                access to compute resources, data, and tools. How do we fit
                these new things in? We've often had a coordination problem in
                these areas. As humans, we are doing the best we can, trying to
                make data simpler, more accessible, and easier to understand.
                But fundamentally, there are many things going on, and most of
                us have to manage many different details all at once.
            </p>
            <p>
                I go back to this idea of cognitive offloading. For all we
                think about AI, we consider how we are offloading our thoughts
                to AI, to the systems around it, and to the machine learning
                models that represent the data better. But the flip side is
                that we haven't thought enough about how to be more critical
                and deliberate: how, within the relationality we choose to
                manage our issues, we decide who we want to do more for.
            </p>
            <p>
                Going back to the home example, this can include local
                observations and evidence. If you know you might have an issue
                because systems are designed better to inform you, or if you
                have sensors installed on your house, who owns those? Do you
                understand the data yourself? Regarding surveys, specialized
                satellite data is available, but it comes down to this core
                concept: when there is a finding about something important to
                you, how have you ensured that the system flags it so somebody
                tells you, while you retain the ability to question it and know
                how to do so?
            </p>
            <p>
                It circles back to the idea of authority. Often, simple surveys
                may be public, but who publishes the findings? Who decides what
                happens next?
            </p>
            <p>
                When I think about Canada in 2080, there are many things to
                consider when designing systems right now because there are
                opportunities for new frameworks. However, there are many
                commonalities with the conversations we are having in Alaska.
                When thinking about these systems, especially the geospatial
                components, we've been designing new information and knowledge
                networks directly inspired by community conversations that have
                already taken place. We have been thinking about the
                intersections between the socioeconomic environment and
                institutional connections.
            </p>
            <p>
                One thing we've been doing in our work is thinking about how to
                formalize community planning measures that need to be embedded
                in our information systems going forward. As a start, we have
                been including tests focused on four elements of control.
            </p>
            <p>
                Contestability. Can affected communities, individuals, or
                institutions contest or refuse consequential decisions? This is
                a key starting point.
            </p>
            <p>
                Dependency. Can participants operate, interpret, maintain, and
                negotiate without relying on a single vendor or expert? In
                other words, how resilient is the system?
            </p>
            <p>
                Value intention. Are community assets strengthened or
                extracted, and what does that look like?
            </p>
            <p>
                Adaptability. Can institutions and communities exit, provide,
                substitute, or repair?
            </p>
            <p>
                A lot of thought has gone into these types of planning
                frameworks. If I can offer one suggestion, it's that it is easy
                to look at all the data and feel overwhelmed, but you should
                also view it as an area of opportunity. When you start from how
                you want your systems to function, what actions those systems
                perform, and the tacit knowledge people hold to define the
                problems, it becomes much easier to organize the data.
                Especially with our use of AI right now, we can perform much
                better planning. These are key opportunities for Canada in
                envisioning what the future looks like. Thank you.
            </p>
        </blockquote>
